---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeHash from '@/components/tools/security/DevEncodeHash.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="crc32-calculator">
  <DevEncodeHash client:load algorithm="CRC32" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>A checksum, not a hash</h2>
    <p>
      CRC-32 catches accidents: a flipped bit on a wire, a bad sector, a truncated transfer. It does
      not resist attacks: a different file with the same CRC takes seconds to make, with no special
      hardware. Use it against physical errors, and a real hash against people.
    </p>

    <h2>Which variant this is</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Value</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Polynomial</td><td>0x04C11DB7, reflected as 0xEDB88320</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Initial value</td><td>0xFFFFFFFF</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Final XOR</td><td>0xFFFFFFFF</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Input / output reflected</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Known as</td><td>CRC-32, CRC-32/ISO-HDLC, &ldquo;the zip CRC&rdquo;</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      ZIP, gzip, PNG, Ethernet and most tools that report a &ldquo;CRC32&rdquo; use this variant.
      CRC-32C (Castagnoli, polynomial 0x1EDC6F41) is a different function, used by iSCSI, ext4 and
      SSE4.2 hardware instructions. Its values do not match these, and this is expected.
    </p>

    <h2>Why ZIP tools show a different number</h2>
    <p>
      The CRC in a ZIP entry is calculated over the uncompressed contents of that one member, not over
      the archive file. So the CRC of <code>archive.zip</code> as a file is not the CRC that your
      archiver lists for <code>document.txt</code> inside it. Extract the member first, then calculate
      its CRC here, and the values agree.
    </p>

    <h2>What CRC-32 is genuinely good at</h2>
    <p>
      Its guarantees are precise. A CRC with a well-chosen 32-bit polynomial detects all single-bit
      errors, all double-bit errors within a very long span, all odd numbers of bit errors, and all
      bursts of errors up to 32 bits, with certainty. Beyond that, it misses about one in four billion
      random corruptions.
    </p>
    <p>
      For this reason it is used at the bottom of the stack: in Ethernet frames, in each PNG chunk, in
      each ZIP entry. It is cheap enough for hardware to calculate at line rate and strong enough for
      the errors that real wires and disks make.
    </p>

    <h2>Hex or decimal</h2>
    <p>
      The value is a 32-bit unsigned integer. Most tools print it as eight lowercase hex characters;
      some libraries return the same number in decimal, and a few, in languages with signed 32-bit
      integers, show it as a negative number. The page shows all forms, so you can match the one you
      have.
    </p>

    <h2>Streaming, so size is not an issue</h2>
    <p>
      CRC-32 updates one 32-bit register as bytes arrive, so this CRC32 calculator reads a file in
      slices and holds only one chunk at a time. A 20&nbsp;GB archive is as easy as a text file, and
      nothing is uploaded, so there is no transfer to wait for.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
